<ResourceDictionary xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:sys="using:System">

  <!--
    Sizes the design states, named by the thing they measure.

    One conversion runs through all of them: the mockup is CSS with content-box sizing, so
    a declared height there excludes its border, while an Avalonia Height includes it. Every
    height below is the Avalonia one - the CSS number plus its borders - and says so where
    the two differ.
  -->

  <!-- Corner radii. Seven values, each tied to a kind of thing rather than a size step. -->
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusSegment">4</CornerRadius>
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusControl">5</CornerRadius>
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusButton">6</CornerRadius>
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusStrip">7</CornerRadius>
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusPanel">8</CornerRadius>
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusTile">9</CornerRadius>
  <!-- The one capsule in the design: a stream chip. -->
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusChip">14</CornerRadius>

  <!--
    The two halves of a control built from two widgets glued at a shared edge - the frame
    rate's typed box and the ladder beside it. They are the control radius with the inner
    corners taken off, so the pair reads as one box rather than as two that happen to touch.
    Stated here rather than written as literals at the joint, for the reason every other
    number in this file is: a radius that moved would move on one side only.
  -->
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusControlLeading">5,0,0,5</CornerRadius>
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusControlTrailing">0,5,5,0</CornerRadius>

  <!--
    Chrome bands. Full-bleed, so their heights set the window's fixed rows.

    Every height in this block and the next grew by 2-5px when the type ladder's default
    went from 13px to 14px (Design/Typography.axaml). A band sized to the old face left a
    14px line box with one or two pixels of air, and a row that tight is exactly where
    vertical centring stops looking centred: the rounding that puts a 17px line box inside
    28px of control has nowhere to go, so the glyphs sit high. The rule is the same one the
    design already followed - the band is sized from the text it holds, not the other way
    round.
  -->
  <sys:Double x:Key="TitleBarHeight">36</sys:Double>
  <!--
    The caption glyphs, and the line they are drawn with. Both numbers are Windows' own:
    minimise, maximise, restore and close are a 10px box at a hairline, and these three stand
    in for the platform's, so they take the platform's measurements rather than a step off
    this design's icon ladder (docs/design-language.md).
  -->
  <sys:Double x:Key="CaptionGlyphSize">10</sys:Double>
  <sys:Double x:Key="CaptionStroke">1</sys:Double>
  <!-- The 30px control it holds, 6px of air either side, plus the 1px bottom rule. -->
  <sys:Double x:Key="NavStripHeight">43</sys:Double>
  <sys:Double x:Key="StatusBarHeight">30</sys:Double>

  <!-- Controls. The 20px line box a 14px label draws, plus 4px either side and two borders. -->
  <sys:Double x:Key="ControlHeight">30</sys:Double>
  <!-- The on-air pill carries no border, so it is the same box two pixels shorter. -->
  <sys:Double x:Key="PillHeight">28</sys:Double>
  <sys:Double x:Key="ChipHeight">30</sys:Double>
  <!--
    A read-only row of text: a config key and its value, a cost metric, a log line. It is
    pinned rather than left to the text because a list of them has to keep one rhythm even
    where one row's value happens to be taller, and it is one number rather than three
    because the three lists were 21, 23 and 24 for no reason anyone could state - the kind
    of near-miss that reads as a wobble down a column.
  -->
  <sys:Double x:Key="TextRowHeight">26</sys:Double>
  <sys:Double x:Key="MenuItemHeight">32</sys:Double>
  <!-- A live-safe action in the broadcast header. -->
  <sys:Double x:Key="ActionHeight">32</sys:Double>
  <!-- A wizard footer button, and the go-live button that matches it. -->
  <sys:Double x:Key="FooterButtonHeight">38</sys:Double>
  <sys:Double x:Key="RoundButtonSize">40</sys:Double>

  <!-- Spacing rhythm. The design uses a small set repeatedly rather than a scale. -->
  <sys:Double x:Key="GapTight">7</sys:Double>
  <sys:Double x:Key="GapControl">10</sys:Double>
  <sys:Double x:Key="GapPanel">12</sys:Double>
  <sys:Double x:Key="GapSection">16</sys:Double>

  <!--
    The same four steps, as the inset of a box rather than the gap between two. A gap is a
    Double and an inset is a Thickness, and a StaticResource converts between neither: a
    Margin handed the Double compiles and then throws InvalidCastException in the first
    layout pass, which is how this second set came to be written down.

    Beside the gaps rather than as a literal at each box, so a step that moves moves in one
    place. The two lines of a step move together, and that is the whole of the duplication.

    Uniform only. A box that needs one edge different - a card with room under it for the
    next card - writes its four numbers where it needs them, because a key per edge pairing
    is a ladder no reader could hold.
  -->
  <Thickness x:Key="InsetTight">7</Thickness>
  <Thickness x:Key="InsetControl">10</Thickness>
  <Thickness x:Key="InsetPanel">12</Thickness>
  <Thickness x:Key="InsetSection">16</Thickness>

  <!-- The state dot stays small: it is state, not decoration (docs/design-language.md). -->
  <sys:Double x:Key="DotSize">7</sys:Double>

  <!--
    The wait. One size for every control that can be waiting, because the spinner replaces a
    label rather than sitting beside one and a spinner scaled per button would make the same
    state read as several. It is the control-glyph size, so a button that is working is the
    same optical weight as one that is not.
  -->
  <sys:Double x:Key="SpinnerSize">14</sys:Double>
  <sys:Double x:Key="SpinnerStroke">1.5</sys:Double>

  <!--
    Icons. Two sizes off the design's 12-22px range, named by where the glyph sits rather
    than by the number, and one stroke for both of them.

    The stroke is a width rather than a weight baked into the artwork, which is the whole
    reason the icons are drawn as paths: a 12px badge glyph and a 14px control glyph are the
    same line, so the two do not read as two different sets (docs/design-language.md).

    The window controls are not on this ladder. They are the platform's glyphs at the
    platform's size, above.
  -->
  <sys:Single x:Key="IconStroke">1.2</sys:Single>
  <!-- Inside a badge or a pill, where the surrounding shape is already 15-16px. -->
  <sys:Double x:Key="IconSizeBadge">12</sys:Double>
  <!-- On a line of text: a select's caret, a menu row's tick. -->
  <sys:Double x:Key="IconSizeControl">14</sys:Double>

  <!--
    A thumbnail in the film strip under a spotlit stream. The height the two existing viewers
    already use for it (docs/viewer-architecture.md), and a height alone: the width follows
    from the shape the grid cuts its cells to, and a short window shrinks the band rather than
    letting it take the spotlight's room.
  -->
  <sys:Double x:Key="StripTileHeight">112</sys:Double>

</ResourceDictionary>
